// @run
// @hint Intentionally-unused parameter
// @hint redundant type annotation: `applyV` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `applyZ` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `applyW` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `applyI` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `zf` is inferred as `(Z) -> i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `runR` is inferred as `i32`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `passZ` is inferred as `i32`
// THE NUMERIC HALF of the un-annotated union-callback PIN, which two predicates cannot see.
// Three lines are the whole repro, and the type declared on the first one is never used:
//
//     type Z = 0 | 1
//     function apply(f: (i32 | string) => i32): i32 { return f(1) }
//     print(apply((_k) => 7))
//   → vl check rc=0, clean; vl build rc=0; vl run → "wasm trap: cast failure"
//
// Move that `type Z` line BELOW a `type V = i32 | string` and the identical program is
// correct. This is `#1270`'s defect verbatim — the pin picked by DECLARATION ORDER, reading
// as a curse on the file rather than on the program — reproduced on the axis its 140-cell
// grid did not carry. `unionParamPinName` skipped a litunion `unNames` row when choosing the
// BOXED pin, and its skip is `nameIsLitUnionType`, which is STRING-ONLY: `tyIsLitUnion`
// rejects any member whose `litKind != "str"`, so every NUMERIC literal union answers "not a
// litunion" and a `type Z = 0 | 1` row was offered as a box it is not.
//
// A NUMERIC litunion is a THIRD rep, not a spelling of either of the other two: a boxed union
// is the shared `{tag, value}` `$uBox`, a STRING litunion is an interned i32 ATOM, and
// `type Z = 0 | 1` is its plain BASE SCALAR (`numLitUnionBaseName` — `i32` / `i64` / `f64`).
// So the selector needs the numeric answer in BOTH of its directions, and the file mirrors
// `#1270`'s pair exactly: this file is the litunion-row-FIRST half, and
// `closure-numeric-litunion-param-pin-boxed-first.vl` is the boxed-row-first half, where the
// numeric PARAM is the one mis-pinned.
//
// WHAT `#1273` ALREADY CLOSED, and why the pin is not covered by it. `#1273` made
// `canonEmitName` canon the INTERIOR of a function type, so `(Z) => i32` reaches the emitter
// as `(i32) => i32` and the two annotation-side `$fnsig` producers (`annParamKind`,
// `annRetKind`) never see the alias at all. That closed the whole FUNC-NESTED family: the
// five-line `run(m: (Z) => i32)` / `print(run((z) => 3))` trap, its `const zf: (Z) => i32`
// value-binding twin, and the `(i32) => Z` result direction. It does NOT reach this selector,
// because canon rewrites the NAMES in annotations and `unionParamPinName` reads neither — it
// reads the registered `unNames` ROWS and the type ARENA. Numeric-litunion arms added to
// `annParamKind` and `annRetKind` were built and MEASURED INERT on top of `#1273`
// (byte-identical on 1,602 corpus files x six channels and on the 120-cell grid below), so
// they were removed rather than shipped; the notes at both sites say so, with the witness.
//
// TWO OUTCOME CLASSES off the one selector, chosen by the lambda BODY: the param UNUSED is a
// cast-failure TRAP (the value call's `ref.cast` finds a scalar where the box was declared),
// and the param USED is INVALID WASM ("type mismatch: expected i32, found (ref $type)", and
// "expected i32, found f64" on the float base, where the mis-pinned lambda feeds a callee that
// took the softened scalar).
//
// THE ANNOTATED TWIN IS A WEAK CONTROL AND MUST NOT BE READ AS A STRONG ONE. It is correct on
// master in every cell below — but for the un-annotated PARAM cells it is correct because the
// annotation lets the HOF specialize to a DIRECT call, which never reaches the value-call ABI
// at all. On the pre-`#1273` master the annotated `(z: Z) => 3` printed 3 while the same
// lambda bound to a `const` first trapped, one type and two routes. `zf` below is kept as the
// binding form for that reason even though `#1273` made it green.
//
// THE GRID, 120 cells: litunion BASE (`0|1` / `1.5|2.5` / `"a"|"b"` / `0|1|null` / `0|"a"`) x
// SPELLING (declared alias / inline members) x DECLARATION ORDER (litunion alone / a boxed
// alias above it / a boxed alias below it) x LAMBDA FORM (param unused / param used) x
// annotated-vs-bare. Master `5770d913` has 12 wrong; all 12 are numeric, all 12 are bare, and
// all 12 are correct now with NO cell backward. They are exactly the two directions of the
// selector that survive `#1273`:
//   · the 4 `boxfirst` `0|1` cells — the numeric PARAM pinned to the first BOXED row.
//   · the 8 `1.5|2.5` cells — the float base, wrong in EVERY declaration order, because the
//     un-pinned param defaults to i32 and the base is f64. (`alone` needs no boxed row to go
//     wrong, which is why the float base is the cell that proves the pin carries the BASE and
//     not merely "not the box".)
// Unchanged: the 24 string cells, the 24 mixed `0|"a"` cells, the 12 nullable-litunion ALIAS
// cells, and every one of the 60 annotated cells.
//
// STILL OPEN, measured and deliberately absent below:
//   · a numeric litunion ARRAY (`type Z = 0 | 1` then `(Z[]) => i32`) is invalid wasm — but
//     `Z[]` does not work in its DIRECT form either (`function take(a: Z[])` is a loud emit
//     error), so it is the element rep that is missing, not this ABI.
//   · an INLINE nullable numeric litunion (`(0 | 1 | null) => i32`) traps, in every
//     declaration order and on master alike. It never reaches this selector — a `TyNullable`
//     is not a `TyUnion`, so it takes the nullable arm one arm down — and it is the numeric
//     twin of the nullable-litunion ALIAS row `#1270` left open.
type Z = 0 | 1
type W = 1.5 | 2.5
type V = i32 | string

function sinkZ(_v: Z) { 2 }

function sinkW(_v: W) { 4 }

function sinkV(_v: i32 | string) { 6 }

// ── THE POISONED BYSTANDER: a genuinely BOXED callback, broken by the numeric row alone ────
// `unNames[0]` is `Z`, and the boxed pin loop skipped only STRING litunion rows — so the
// `(i32 | string)` param was pinned to the numeric alias, whose rep is a plain i32. Delete
// `type Z` from this file, or move it below `type V`, and the identical program is correct on
// master: the input was the declaration order, not the program. (`#1270`'s defect verbatim.)
function applyV(f: (i32 | string) => i32): i32 { return f(1) }

// @log 1
print(applyV((_k) => 1))
// @log 6
print(applyV((k) => sinkV(k)))

// ── the numeric litunion param itself, both bases and both lambda forms ─────────────────────
// The `0|1` cells are GREEN on master in THIS declaration order (`#1273` canons the function
// type's interior, and with no boxed row above it the un-pinned i32 default already agrees
// with the canon'd annotation) — they are here as the order half of the pair, and the mirror
// file carries the order where they red. The `1.5|2.5` cells are NOT: the un-pinned default is
// i32 and the base is f64, so the float base reds here with no boxed row in sight.
function applyZ(f: (Z) => i32): i32 { return f(1) }

// @log 3
print(applyZ((_z) => 3))
// @log 2
print(applyZ((z) => sinkZ(z)))

function applyW(f: (W) => i32): i32 { return f(1.5) }

// @log 7
print(applyW((_z) => 7))
// @log 4
print(applyW((z) => sinkW(z)))

// The INLINE spelling needs no split from the alias one, unlike the string half: both soften
// to the SAME base scalar (an alias name that is not an atom identity has nothing to
// preserve), so `numLitUnionBaseName` answers once for both.
function applyI(f: (0 | 1) => i32): i32 { return f(1) }

// @log 9
print(applyI((_z) => 9))

// ── THE VALUE BINDING and THE RESULT TWIN — both `#1273`'s, both kept as CONTROLS ──────────
// Neither reds on `5770d913`, and both are recorded here rather than dropped because each was
// a live invalid module one commit ago and each is the shape a future change to this selector
// would break first. The binding is what forces the value call that HOF specialization hides
// from the annotated form; the result direction is reachable ONLY through a NAMED function,
// because every LAMBDA spelling of a `(i32) => Z` callback is rejected by the CHECKER
// ("expected (i32) -> 0 | 1, got (i32) -> i32") — which is exactly what made that position
// look unreachable when it was not. Checking a twin is not the same as checking one SPELLING
// of the twin.
const zf: (Z) => i32 = (_z: Z) => 11

// @log 11
print(applyZ(zf))

function mkZ(_n: i32): Z { 1 }

function runR(m: (i32) => Z): i32 {
  const r = m(1)
  const s = r + 4
  return s
}

// @log 5
print(runR(mkZ))

// ── HIGHER ORDER: the callback's own callback carries the alias ─────────────────────────────
function passZ(g: ((Z) => i32) => i32): i32 { return g((_z) => 13) }

// @log 13
print(passZ(applyZ))

// ── CONTROLS: the specialized annotated twins, correct on master and unchanged ─────────────
// @log 3
print(applyZ((_z: Z) => 3))
// @log 1
print(applyV((_k: i32 | string) => 1))
// @log 7
print(applyW((_z: W) => 7))
